//4. left rotate array by one:

//Given an integer array nums, rotate the array to the left by one.
Note: There is no need to return anything, just modify the given array.
Example 1:
Input: nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Output: [2, 3, 4, 5, 1]
Explanation:
Initially, nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5]
Rotating once to left -> nums = [2, 3, 4, 5, 1]


//solution:
class Solution {
public:
    void rotateArrayByOne(vector<int>& nums) {
        int n=nums.size();
        int temp=nums[0];
        for(int i=1;i<n;i++){
            nums[i-1]=nums[i];
        }
        nums[n-1]=temp;
        
    }
};
// Left rotation of an array by one place ka matlab hai har element ko ek position left shift karna aur jo first 
element beginning se bahar jaata hai, usko array ke last position par place karna. Sabse pehle n = nums.size() 
se array ka size calculate karte hain, phir int temp = nums[0] se first element ko temporary variable mein store 
karte hain, kyunki shifting ke time first element overwrite ho jayega. Uske baad for(int i=1; i<n; i++) loop se 
remaining elements ko ek position left shift karte hain using nums[i-1] = nums[i], jisse second element first position 
par, third element second position par aur aise hi saare elements left shift ho jaate hain. Finally, nums[n-1] = temp 
se previously stored first element ko array ke last position par place kar dete hain. For example, {10,20,30,40,50} ko 
left rotate by one karne par final array {20,30,40,50,10} milega. Is approach ki Time Complexity O(n) aur Auxiliary Space 
Complexity O(1) hai, kyunki hum sirf ek temporary variable use kar rahe hain.

